[CourseDescription "This is the 'Best Beginner Chess Opening' and for good reason. The plans are easy to learn and there are so many traps for Black to lose in 10 moves."]
[Chapter "Jaenisch Counterattack (3...Nf6)"]
[Study "Black Takes On d4 (4...exd4)"]
[Video "/downloader/downloads/The Ponziani/01-01 Jaenisch Counterattack (3...Nf6) - Black Takes On d4 (4...exd4).mp4"]
[Orientation "white"]

{Welcome to the first study of our Ponziani course. In Chapter 1, we'll examine the Jaenisch counterattack, which is the most popular response for Black after 3.c3. The idea is that Black plays 3...Nf6 to attack e4. Study 1 will deal with Black capturing our pawn on d4 after we advance in the center.}
1. e4 e5 {Black responds with ...e5, the most popular first move for Black! The knight comes out to its natural f3-square and attacks the center.} 2. Nf3 Nc6 {Black develops and defends e5. We play c3, the Ponziani, with the idea of playing d4 next. This in turn can offer open lines and extremely active piece play down the line. A downside could be that the c3-square is now occupied and we can't place a knight there... [%csl Re5]} 3. c3 Nf6 {Black plays a rather natural move, attacking the center, which takes advantage of the fact that we can't defend the e4-pawn with our knight. The Jaenisch Counterattack is played 45% of the time, in almost 8 million games. We'll gambit a pawn on e4, but we obtain a strong center and put pressure on Black's e5-pawn. [%csl Re4]} 4. d4 exd4 {This study will deal with the most popular response for Black in this position, which is capturing on d4, played 66% of the time. We'll unleash an intermezzo and push in the center with tempo! This move takes advantage of the extra space immediately, prevents Black from capturing on c3 or on e4, and forces Black's knight to move!} 5. e5 Nd5 {Played 32% of the time and in almost 600,000 games, 5...Nd5 is the most popular response for Black. This move is played with a concrete goal of keeping the knight safe but active. Capturing on d4 now could be played, but we give Black some chances after ...Bb4+. Instead, we attack the knight with Bc4...} (5... Ng4 {5...Ng4 looks aggressive and is the second most popular move played 22% of the time. It seems like Black is targeting the e5-pawn, but this gives us the freedom we strive for in the Ponziani. [%csl Re5]} 6. cxd4 {We'll simply capture on d4, protect the pawn and maintain a massive center. We'll play h3 next to kick the g4-knight away and already achieve a substantial advantage. [%cal Gh2h3] [%csl Gd4,Ge5,Rg4]}) (5... Qe7 {Placing the queen on e7 early, in front of the bishop, seems illogical, but it is the third most popular move here, played 16% of the time. Black pins our pawn on e5 to the king. Capturing in the center is simple and natural. We take our pawn back and protect the center. [%csl Ge7,Re5,Re1]} 6. cxd4 d6 {Black can open up the light-squared bishop and put pressure on e5. It's impractical to defend the pawn with Bf4 now, so I suggest something else. Bb5 essentially serves the same purpose as protecting the pawn on e5, as Black has one less attacker on it now. [%csl Re5]} 7. Bb5 dxe5 {They can still capture once, however. We'll take back...} 8. dxe5 Bd7 {And now Black unpins the knight on c6. It seems like our only options to save the pawn are to play Bf4, which is rather passive, or to capture the knight on c6. However, we can castle our king! The point is that Black can't afford to capture the pawn now because of Re1 problems, and now the threat of capturing on f6 is reintroduced.} 9. O-O Ng4 {That's why jumping to g4 is the most logical for Black. We don't have to worry about the e5-pawn for now, though.} 10. Bg5 {We can simply develop our dark-squared bishop with tempo on the queen, and Black is in trouble. Blocking with ...f6 leads to a disaster on the e-file after we capture, so Black has to play something like ...Qc5. After that, we'll simply play Nc3 and enjoy an easy position and a winning advantage. [%cal Gb1c3,Gf1e1,Ge7c5,Ga1c1] [%csl Re7]}) (5... Ne4 {...Ne4 is played 14% of the time on move 5. Black wants an active knight in the center and wants to play ...d5 to support it. Of course, we can easily attack that knight while developing a piece, which is more often than not a good choice in the opening.} 6. Qc2 d5 {Black goes ahead with their plan and protects the knight with ...d5. Here, Black would like to play ...Bf5, which is why I don't recommend cxd4. Let's instead develop the bishop and pin Black's knight on c6. This way we threaten to capture on d4 with the knight in case Black plays ...Bf5. [%csl Gd5,Ge4]} 7. Bb5 dxc3 {That's why Black removes our pawn on c3. We'll recapture with the knight in order to activate another piece. Now Black's knight on e4 is under additional pressure. Once again, if Black tries ...Bf5, Nd4 is strong.} 8. Nxc3 Nxc3 {You will see a trade on c3 here most likely. We'll take back with the queen to keep the pawns together and attack the knight on c6. [%csl Rc6]} 9. Qxc3 Bd7 {The most natural defense is with the bishop on d7. This gives us the freedom to castle.} 10. O-O Be7 {Black continues developing naturally, preparing to castle themselves. However, this bishop left its post on f8 and isn't guarding the g7-pawn anymore... Which means that we can push e6! Keep this trick in mind when there's a queen on c3. We give up the e-pawn in exchange for the g7-pawn. [%csl Rg7]} 11. e6 fxe6 {Black has no choice but to capture on e6 as the bishop was under attack as well. And now we'll take on g7 and hit the rook in the corner. [%csl Rg7]} 12. Qxg7 Bf6 {There's an active defense for Black in the form of ...Bf6.} 13. Qg3 {However, placing the queen on g3 is convenient for us, as it's safe there. Black most likely won't be castling kingside. For the middlegame, active and natural moves are Bf4 to line up with the queen on g3 and hit the c7-pawn, and bringing the rooks to the center. All in all, an interesting but equal position. [%cal Gc1f4,Gf1e1,Ga1d1] [%csl Gg3,Rc7]}) 6. Bc4 Nb6 {It can rotate to the queenside and hit our bishop. The b3-square is where we want to retreat to most of the time. We did give Black a tempo in this small skirmish, through our bishop retreat. 7...Be7 allows us the calm 8.O-O, and we have ideas of playing 9.cxd4. [%csl Rc4]} 7. Bb3 dxc3 {Our retreat allows them to capture our pawn on c3. That's fine. We'll recapture with the knight. [%cal Gb1c3]} (7... d5 {With this move there are nearly 3,000 games. Black wants central space. We will take on d4 and get a big center.} 8. cxd4 Bg4 {8...Bg4 pins our knight. And tries to undermine our defense of d4. We will support d4 further. [%csl Rf3,Rd4]} 9. Be3 Bb4+ {Black speeds up development with this check. But we can develop and block. [%csl Re1]} 10. Nc3 O-O {Black castles. We would love to get that bishop pair.} 11. h3 Bh5 {But Black will usually go backwards to h5 and maintain the pin.} 12. O-O {And now we castle: we will go g4 and Qe2 to connect our rooks. We should be happy with our strong central pawns. [%cal Gg2g4,Gd1e2]}) (7... d6 {There are more than 2,000 games with 7...d6. Black wants to undermine e5. We will simply castle and let them take on e5 if they want. We have e-file counterplay. If 8...Nxe5, we want to play 9.Nxe5 and then cxd4. We want to open up lines towards the king in the center. [%csl Re5]} 8. O-O dxe5 {If the pawn takes on e5, we don't mind. Our goal is to open up lines and attack f7 and potentially the e-file. We can begin to attack f7. [%csl Rf7]} (8... Be7 {8...Be7 is the solid way of developing. We will simply take on d6. [%csl Rd6]} 9. exd6 Qxd6 {The queen recaptures so the bishop doesn't move again. And we take on d4 with the knight. We wanted to avoid Bg4.} 10. Nxd4 Nxd4 {Material is equal in this line. But we will have an isolated queen's pawn.} 11. cxd4 O-O {Black castles.} 12. Nc3 {We will try to limit trading pieces and use our pieces to move around the IQP. With the knight on c3 and bishop on b3, we can even consider pushing d5 safely. [%cal Gf1e1,Gd4d5,Gd1f3] [%csl Ge5,Gc5]}) 9. Ng5 Nd5 {Black can block the attack on f7 with their knight. However, we will simply open up lines towards their king.} (9... Be6 {The bishop can block. We will take the bishop and get a pawn back. [%csl Re6]} 10. Nxe6 fxe6 {Black recaptures. We take on e6.} 11. Bxe6 dxc3 {Black can get greedy. But open lines are what we want. Pawns aren't important in this variation: rather, getting our pieces active is what we must do.} 12. Qg4 cxb2 {If Black continues taking pawns, we have so much activity that the engine says we're equal.} 13. Bxb2 {We recapture with the bishop. We will play Nc3 and Rad1 and Rfe1. Ne4 could also be a great follow-up to Nc3. [%cal Gb1c3,Ga1d1,Gf1e1,Gc3e4]}) 10. cxd4 Nxd4 {10...Nxd4 is a solid approach. The centralized knight keeps the e-file closed for the time being, and it prevents our Qf3 move. We will make use of the e-file to counterattack and claim some compensation.} (10... exd4 {If the pawn recaptures, we've got a nice double attack.} 11. Qf3 {We are completely winning here. [%csl Rd5,Rf7]}) 11. Re1 Nxb3 {This trade gives up a pawn. However, Black's point is that they're getting rid of our powerful bishop. We take the pawn with check.} 12. Rxe5+ Be6 {The bishop blocks, lending extra defense to the knight on d5 in the process. [%csl Gd5]} 13. Qxb3 {The engine suggest we give a check on b5 if we can before taking on b7. That's because we want to provoke Qd7 so that Qxb7 comes with a tempo. We have good open lines and we will also trade our knight for that bishop which is currently holding everything together. [%cal Gg5e6,Gb3b5,Gb5b7] [%csl Rd5,Rb7,Re6]}) 8. Nxc3 Bb4 {One of the logical moves for Black is to pin our knight on c3. Let's just bring the king to safety. [%csl Rc3]} 9. O-O O-O {Black chooses to do the same. Since they haven't captured the c3-knight now... We can choose to protect it with the queen and potentially avoid our pawns splitting. However, there is an additional point behind this move...} (9... Bxc3 {Black capturing on c3 can actually lead them into trouble. We'll take back first.} 10. bxc3 O-O {If they try to play logically and castle, thinking they have no worries... Suddenly Bg5 arrives! This is why it's important for Black to move the d-pawn before this.} 11. Bg5 Qe8 {The queen's only available square is e8.} 12. Re1 {After which we can play Re1. We've got a big lead in activity and development and a winning advantage. This initiative could transform into either an attack on the king, or a slow positional build up until we squeeze Black. [%csl Ge5]}) 10. Qd3 d6 {Expect Black to move the d-pawn at some point and challenge your e5-pawn. However, ...d6 in this case is a mistake! Remember that you can push the pawn to e6 in this position. Why is this possible? [%csl Re5]} (10... Bxc3 {If Black captures on c3, we won't recapture with the queen immediately. We'll play Ng5 first and threaten checkmate! [%csl Rh7]} 11. Ng5 g6 {Black can defend against it, but the point of this line is to weaken Black's pawn structure. We can additionally do that here with Qh3.} 12. Qh3 h5 {Black is forced to play ...h5 if they don't want to give up the queen.} 13. Qxc3 {And only now can we capture on c3. After this combination, Black's kingside structure is extremely weak and the g6-pawn isn't even defended by the f7-pawn, as it's pinned. We'll look to activate the rest of the pieces and target those weak spots. White's got a big advantage here. [%csl Rf7,Rg6,Rh5]}) 11. e6 Bxe6 {Once Black captures on e6... There's the extremely strong Ng5. Not only do we attack the bishop twice, but we also threaten checkmate on h7! [%csl Re6,Rh7]} 12. Ng5 g6 {Black has to defend against checkmate, of course. Now we'll cash in on the e6-square.} 13. Bxe6 fxe6 {Black recaptures. And we'll take with the knight and a fork!} 14. Nxe6 Qh4 {Black tries to play actively with ...Qh4.} 15. Nxf8 {But we've just won an exchange and have a huge advantage. Try to remember this e6 trick when you've got the pieces set up in the right way.} *

[Chapter "Jaenisch Counterattack (3...Nf6)"]
[Study "Black Plays 4...Nxe4"]
[Video "/downloader/downloads/The Ponziani/01-02 Jaenisch Counterattack (3...Nf6) - Black Plays 4...Nxe4.mp4"]
[Orientation "white"]

{We're continuing our analysis of the Jaenisch Counterrattack. This study will deal with Black capturing our e4-pawn with their knight after we advance in the center. Let's get to it!}
1. e4 e5 {Black responds with ...e5, the most popular response to 1. e4. We develop our kingside knight to the most natural square, attacking e5.} 2. Nf3 Nc6 {Black develops their knight and defends the central pawn. And we go for the Ponziani with 3. c3. The idea is, once again, to prepare a quick central expansion with d4. [%csl Re5]} 3. c3 Nf6 {Black responds by developing their other knight to f6 and attacking e4. This move signifies the Jaenisch Counterattack, which is the most popular response to the Ponziani. Black's move intends to make use of the fact that we no longer have a convenient way to support e4. But instead of supporting e4, we'll follow up on our previous move and strike in the center with d4! [%csl Re4]} 4. d4 Nxe4 {This study will deal with the second most popular response on move 4, which happens 14% of the time. This capture often leads to some incredibly sharp and dynamic positions where a high degree of accuracy is required. Instead of taking on e5, we'll advance with d5, taking some more space and attacking Black's knight!} 5. d5 Ne7 {By far the most common response is 5...Ne7, played 55% of the time. Black's knight was attacked, so they move it back to e7 with the intent to bring it over to g6 at some point. Seems like a safe choice, but Black is still far from safety in this position... We'll continue by simply capturing the en prise e5-pawn with our knight. [%csl Re5]} (5... Bc5 {Played 16% of the time is the ultra sharp piece sacrifice with 5...Bc5! This is where you need to know your stuff. One wrong move from either side can result in total devastation. We will accept Black's offering. Luckily, Black is the one who is going to have to prove that they have got enough compensation for the piece. [%csl Rf2]} 6. dxc6 Bxf2+ {Most often, Black takes on f2 with their bishop and checks our king. Our only legal move is Ke2. A bongcloud, but up a piece. [%csl Re1]} (6... Nxf2 {Played roughly 36% of the time is Black's only other option, ...Nxf2. This really isn't all that great, but you need to know how to punish it. We'll move our queen to d5. Similarly to the ...Bxf2+ lines, this move attacks a piece of Black's (the bishop in this case) and allows for a potential cxb7 at the right moment. [%csl Rh1,Rd1]} 7. Qd5 Bb6 {Black can retreat the bishop to safety. We'll respond by saving our rook. [%csl Rh1]} (7... Qe7 {Black can also defend the bishop. Against this, it's best to capture the b7-pawn and fork Black's pieces! [%csl Rb7]} 8. cxb7 Bxb7 {Black has to capture the b7-pawn. We'll capture the bishop. Similar to the ...Bxf2+ line, we need to keep analyzing to know how to best deal with Black's strong initiative.} 9. Qxb7 O-O {Black's best is to castle kingside. We'll go Rg1, saving the rook. [%csl Rh1]} 10. Rg1 e4 {Black advances their e-pawn and attacks our knight. The idea is to dislodge the knight from f3 and free up the h4-square. We'll go Nd4, saving the knight. [%csl Rf3]} 11. Nd4 Qh4 {Black moves their queen into h4, threatening some discoveries. We'll respond with g3, attacking the queen and blocking the diagonal. [%csl Gh4,Re1]} 12. g3 Qxh2 {Black captures the h2-pawn with their queen and attacks our rook. We'll go Rg2, saving our rook and attacking Black's knight and queen. [%csl Rg1]} 13. Rg2 Nd3+ {Black has to give a check with their knight in order not to lose material. We can now slide our king over to d1. [%csl Re1]} 14. Kd1 Qh1 {And when Black moves their queen to h1 and attacks our bishop... [%csl Rf1]} 15. Nd2 {We are just in time with Nd2, defending the bishop on f1. White is up material (two pieces for three pawns) and is much better. We're a little tangled up, but our plan to untangle is Re2, followed by Kc2. The natural-looking ...e3 would now allow us to simply retreat our queen. [%cal Gg2e2,Gd1c2] [%csl Gf1]}) 8. Rg1 bxc6 {Black's best is to capture the c6-pawn, preventing any cxb7 ideas. We'll respond by taking the e5-pawn. [%csl Rd5]} 9. Qxe5+ Kf8 {Black really shouldn't trade queens, so the best course of action is ...Kf8.} 10. Nd4 {And we can jump our knight into d4. We're up a piece for two pawns and Black doesn't have a clear attack or way to regain the material.}) 7. Ke2 Bb6 {Usually, Black retreats the bishop. We'll move our queen to d5. This move attacks Black's knight, but also potentially enables us to capture the b7-pawn at the right moment.} (7... bxc6 {Black can also capture the c6-pawn. This is played roughly 28% of the time and isn't all that great for Black. We'll proceed with Qa4, activating our queen and attacking the e4-knight, also stopping ...Ba6+. [%cal Rc8a6]} 8. Qa4 f5 {The knight can't move because it's defending the f2-bishop, so Black must defend it by advancing ...f5. We can now go Nbd2, simply looking to trade off Black's key knight on e4.} 9. Nbd2 Nxd2 {It is logical for Black to trade knights.} 10. Bxd2 {And we'll recapture with our bishop. White is crushing here. The bishop and the e5-pawn are hanging, and Bg5 is a serious threat to win Black's queen. [%cal Rd2g5] [%csl Re5,Rf2]}) 8. Qd5 Nf2 {Black proceeds with ...Nf2, saving their knight and attacking our rook. We'll go Rg1, saving the rook. [%csl Rh1]} 9. Rg1 O-O {Black's best is to castle kingside. This is another piece sacrifice... We can capture the b7-pawn and fork! [%csl Rb7]} 10. cxb7 Bxb7 {Black has to recapture the b7-pawn. And we'll capture the bishop. We are up a bishop and a knight, but our analysis can't stop here. Black has a strong initiative and we need to know the best way to deal with it.} 11. Qxb7 Qf6 {Black activates their queen. ...e4 is coming next and our king could run into some trouble... The best way to proceed is Na3. Our defensive idea is to get the knight to c4 and cover up our king with Be3. [%cal Re5e4] [%csl Re2]} 12. Na3 e4 {Black advances their e-pawn and attacks our knight. Trying to hang onto the f3-knight is too dangerous. Instead, we will bring the a3-knight to help defend the king. [%csl Rf3]} 13. Nc4 exf3+ {Black captures the knight with check. We ought to recapture with the queen.} 14. Qxf3 Rae8+ {And when Black gives a check... [%csl Re2]} 15. Be3 {We are just in time to cover the king up with our bishop. At the end of this forced mess, the engine gives total equality, but it's Black who needs to play accurately in order to prove that this really is equal. It's easy for Black to go wrong and White has great practical chances. After all, we are up a whole piece!}) (5... Nb8 {This retreat is played 18% of the time, but it's Black admitting that they are worse. We can take the pawn. [%csl Re5]} 6. Nxe5 Qe7 {Black could try to set something up on the e-file. But we can defend our knight. [%csl Ge7,Re5,Re1]} 7. Qd4 Nf6 {Black pins our knight. [%csl Re5]} 8. Be3 {But we can unpin. We will develop with Bc4 and Nd2, with a small advantage thanks to the extra space and development. [%cal Gb1d2,Gf1c4]}) 6. Nxe5 d6 {Shockingly, the most natural way to proceed is a horrible blunder! 6...d6 is played 63% of the time and lands Black into a dead lost position. It's sort of logical as Black attacks the knight and opens up their bishop. But we can punish Black for opening up the diagonal to their king by giving a check with our bishop!} (6... Ng6 {...Ng6 is clearly the better option and is played roughly 16% of the time. Black wants to trade the knights and continue with natural development. Against this, I suggest we play Qe2 and attack the knight on e4. [%csl Re5]} 7. Qe2 Nxe5 {Most often, Black takes the e5-knight. We'll capture the e4-knight.} (7... Qe7 {The other option is ...Qe7. This is slightly less popular, but still played roughly 33% of the time. We'll proceed by taking the g6-knight. [%csl Re5]} 8. Nxg6 hxg6 {Black recaptures with the h-pawn. We should then continue by developing our bishop to e3.} 9. Be3 d6 {Black's most logical way to continue is ...d6, opening up the c8-bishop.} 10. Na3 {And we'll prepare queenside castling with Na3. The knight can jump into c4 or b5 at some point in the future. White is slightly better. We have more space and a lead in development. [%cal Ge1c1,Ga3c4,Ga3b5]}) 8. Qxe4 Qe7 {Black now has to proceed with ...Qe7, defending and unpinning the knight. We can continue naturally developing with Be2, preparing kingside castling.} 9. Be2 d6 {Black's most logical way to continue is ...d6, supporting the knight and opening up the light-squared bishop. [%csl Gd6,Ge5]} 10. O-O {And we can castle our king. White is slightly better here. We have more space and superior development. We can look to proceed with moves like Na3 and Re1, and should avoid any queen trades if possible. [%cal Gb1a3,Gf1e1]}) 7. Bb5+ c6 {The only reasonable way to proceed is to block the check with ...c6. We'll continue by taking the c6-pawn. We are now threatening to win lots of material with cxb7+, so Black has to try to do something about it... [%csl Rc6]} 8. dxc6 bxc6 {If Black simply recaptures... We can capture the c6-pawn with our knight, threatening more discoveries.} (8... Qb6 {A tricky attempt is ...Qb6, attacking the b5-bishop and threatening mate. This move, however, fails miserably and for quite a beautiful reason. We can capture the b7-pawn and uncover a check from our bishop. If Black now takes the bishop we can simply take on a8 and make a queen. [%csl Rf2,Rb5]} 9. cxb7+ Kd8 {Black can attempt one final trick with ...Kd8. Make sure not to prematurely capture the rook and blunder mate! There are many ways to win here, but one is more beautiful than the others. We capture on f7 with our knight.} 10. Nxf7+ Kc7 {Black's only legal move is ...Kc7.} 11. bxa8=N+ {And we finally capture the rook on a8, underpromoting our pawn to a knight and forking Black's king and queen! Black should resign here. [%csl Rc7,Rb6]}) 9. Nxc6 Qb6 {Black's best try is to go for ...Qb6. This attacks the b5-bishop and threatens checkmate in one. Looks scary, but we have a move to deal with both of these threats. [%csl Rf2,Rb5]} 10. Nd4+ {We can simply bring our knight back to d4, defending the bishop and opening up a discovered check. A completely winning position. We are up a pawn and ready to castle. Whatever Black does, they'll be left with a weak king in the center of the board that's subject to attacks. [%cal Ge1g1,Gd4b5] [%csl Gb5,Re8]} *

[Chapter "Jaenisch Counterattack (3...Nf6)"]
[Study "Black Plays 4...d6"]
[Video "/downloader/downloads/The Ponziani/01-03 Jaenisch Counterattack (3...Nf6) - Black Plays 4...d6.mp4"]
[Orientation "white"]

{Now we conclude our introduction to the Ponziani with a short study in which we see some lines that may result in disaster. These lines contain the early pawn to d6 on move 4.}
1. e4 e5 {Black plays e5. We attack the pawn.} 2. Nf3 Nc6 {They defend. And we go for the Ponziani.} 3. c3 Nf6 {The knight goes to f6. And we play 4.d4, with a powerful center. We've seen variations with 4...Nxe4 and ...exd4. [%csl Re4]} 4. d4 d6 {But 4...d6 defends e5. It has been played in more than half a million games, which amounts to 12% of the time. Our e-pawn is hanging, but we can afford to play 5.h3 and prevent ...Bg4. You will see why. [%csl Gd6,Ge5,Re4]} 5. h3 exd4 {Most games go like this. In particular, 36%. We simply recapture.} (5... Nxe4 {5...Nxe4 right away is a blunder. And it's played almost a quarter of the time!} 6. d5 {In this variation, there is no ...Qe7. We will win a knight. [%cal Gd1a4] [%csl Rc6,Re8,Re4]}) (5... Be7 {Finally, there's always normal development, which happens 23% of the time. Black wants to follow opening principles and castle quickly. We will gain space and attack the knight. [%cal Ge8g8]} 6. d5 Nb8 {The bishop went to e7, so the knight doesn't have that square. Finally, we defend our e4-pawn. [%csl Re4]} 7. Bd3 O-O {Black castles. They have a position that looks solid, but with less space.} 8. c4 {We will play O-O, Nc3 and Be3 to develop. We will use our greater space advantage to play on both sides of the board. [%cal Gb1c3,Gc1e3,Ge1g1,Ga1c1]}) 6. cxd4 Nxe4 {If the knight takes what seems to be a free pawn...} 7. d5 {We attack the knight on c6 and get ready to play the fork with Qa4+. The only move to save the game is the complex 7...Qe7. After which we will block the e-file with 8.Be2 and then play Qa4+, followed by 0-0 and getting a rook to the recently opened e-file. Despite the pawn, we have an advantage. [%cal Gd8e7,Gf1e2,Gd1a4,Ge1g1] [%csl Rc6]} *

[Chapter "Black's Other 3rd Moves"]
[Study "3...Bc5 and 3...d6"]
[Video "/downloader/downloads/The Ponziani/02-01 Black's Other 3rd Moves - 3...Bc5 and 3...d6.mp4"]
[Orientation "white"]

{Welcome to the second chapter of the Ponziani! Let's take a look at Black's third move options which are 3...Bc5, controlling the d4-square and 3...d6, going for a more solid approach.}
1. e4 e5 {Black replies symmetrically. We develop.} 2. Nf3 Nc6 {Black develops and protects e5. With c3 we go for The Ponziani, preparing to expand quickly with d4 and gain control over the center.} 3. c3 Bc5 {Black abandons the idea of developing the knight with 3...Nf6, but rather controls the d4-square by bringing the bishop out to c5. This is played more than 25% of the time. Let's push nonetheless! We open up the dark-squared bishop and gain tempo on Black's c5-bishop. [%csl Rd4]} (3... d6 {Black chooses to go for a more solid approach with 3...d6, supporting the e5-pawn and opening up the light-squared bishop. This happens in around 13% of the games. Let's push here. c3 followed by d4 is always the plan. [%cal Gc8g4] [%csl Gd6,Ge5]} 4. d4 exd4 {Black trades, which is the most common continuation, happening in more than half of the games. Recapturing with the pawn to build the center and free up the c3-square.} (4... Bg4 {Black immediately pins our knight, the second most popular move after ...exd4, happening in 30% of the games. In this line, it's important to play quick and aggressive moves and put as much pressure on Black's pieces as we can while developing. Let's gain space in the center. [%csl Rf3]} 5. d5 Nce7 {Black retreats. Asking a question to the bishop on g4.} 6. h3 Bh5 {Black keeps the pin. We free up the c3-square and support the d5-pawn. We'll be able to get the knight to c3 on the next move.} 7. c4 Nf6 {Black develops and attacks the pawn. White develops as well. [%csl Re4]} 8. Nc3 c6 {Black starts to chip away at our central structure. We develop the bishop. [%csl Rd5]} 9. Be3 cxd5 {Black trades. We recapture.} 10. cxd5 Qa5 {Black pins our knight on c3, which means that the e4-pawn is hanging. We develop with a check. This tempo move is crucial so that we can castle and unpin. [%csl Re4,Rc3]} 11. Bb5+ Kd8 {Black moves the king. After 11...Nd7, we could win material with the neat trick 12.Bxd7+ Kxd7 13.Nxe5+! We get our king into safety.} 12. O-O Nxe4 {Black uses a tactic to win a pawn... ...we'll gladly trade.} 13. Nxe4 Qxb5 {Black recaptures the piece. [%csl Rd6]} 14. Nxd6 {But we win the pawn back! We've got yet again a great position with many weaknesses in Black's camp including their king. [%cal Ga1c1] [%csl Rd8,Rf7,Rb5,Rb7]}) 5. cxd4 Bg4 {By pinning our knight on f3, Black pressures the d4-pawn. But we simply develop the knight! [%csl Rf3,Rd4]} 6. Nc3 Bxf3 {Black trades. We've damaged our kingside structure, but the strong center proves to be a better asset in this position. Black is underdeveloped.} 7. gxf3 Nf6 {Black develops. Supporting the center and developing a piece.} 8. Be3 Be7 {Black prepares to castle... ...this shows why recapturing on f3 with the g-pawn is an advantage here. Our rook attacks Black's g7-pawn. Castling would be lethal. [%csl Rg7]} 9. Rg1 g6 {Black covers it.} 10. Qb3 {White is doing great here. The b7-pawn is under pressure, and Black's king isn't sure which side to castle on. In the meantime, we can castle queenside and push f4-f5 with a strong grip over the center. [%cal Gf3f4,Ge1c1,Ge3h6,Gf4f5] [%csl Rb7,Re8]}) 4. d4 exd4 {Black trades 93% of the time. We recapture and get the two nice central pawns.} (4... Bb6 {Black retreats the bishop to b6 right away, which rarely happens (only 4% of the time), but should be covered anyway. It is logical as Black keeps the bishop on the g1-a7 diagonal, eyeballing the f2-pawn. We'll push to remove the defender of the e5-pawn! [%csl Rf2]} 5. d5 Nb8 {The knight retreats to b8, hoping to reroute via d7 in the future. Having kicked that knight back, let's gain space on the queenside and threaten to annoy the dark-squared bishop. We'll do this before capturing on e5 because a5 is a serious threat.} 6. a4 a6 {Black frees up the a7-square. Let's grab the e-pawn.} 7. Nxe5 Qe7 {Black attacks the knight, which was their idea all along. We won't be able to save the e4-pawn. But c4 is a nice square for the knight, attacking that bishop. [%csl Re5]} 8. Nc4 Qxe4+ {Black captures. We can safely enter a queenless middlegame, since we are so far ahead in development and have more space.} 9. Qe2 Qxe2+ {Black trades. We recapture.} 10. Bxe2 Bc5 {Black doesn't want to lose the bishop pair. Developing with an attack on the c7-pawn. Developing with tempo is always good.} 11. Bf4 d6 {Black protects. And now we expand on the queenside with a tempo. Now Black has to move their bishop multiple times. And, we are preparing something devious.} 12. b4 Ba7 {Retreating. Out of nowhere, White has a strong piece sacrifice!} 13. Bxd6 cxd6 {Black grabs the piece.} 14. Nxd6+ {Check! Black cannot protect both f7-pawn and the bishop on c7. White is winning. [%csl Rc8,Rf7,Re8]}) 5. cxd4 Bb4+ {Black gives a check. We'll block with the knight. [%csl Re1]} (5... Bb6 {Black retreats the bishop to b6 instead. You know the drill. The c3-square is free so let's develop the queenside knight!} 6. Nc3 d6 {Black opens up the light-squared bishop. We prevent ...Bg4, which would be annoying. [%csl Rg4]} 7. h3 Nf6 {Black develops. We'll develop as well, pinning the knight. The threat is d5. [%csl Rc6]} 8. Bb5 Bd7 {Black breaks the pin. Let's get the king to safety.} 9. O-O O-O {Black castles as well. And we pin the other knight as well. [%csl Rf6]} 10. Bg5 h6 {Black is done allowing these pins, so they attack the bishop. Keeping the pin is important here, though, as it is very annoying for Black. [%csl Rg5]} 11. Bh4 g5 {So they decide to attack the bishop further, but weakening their kingside. Do we have a positional sacrifice here? You guessed it! Let's sac the knight.} 12. Nxg5 hxg5 {Black recaptures.} 13. Bxg5 {And the pin prevails! The king is weak and Nd5 is unstoppable. The Ponziani allows for quick and sharp development, often catching Black off guard. White is winning already. [%cal Gc3d5] [%csl Rf6,Rd8,Rg8]}) 6. Nc3 Nf6 {Black develops the knight and pressures the e4-pawn. There are nearly 300,000 games here. Let's create a bigger threat against Black! We gain space in the center with a tempo. [%csl Re4,Rc3]} (6... d6 {Black opens up the light-squared bishop. There are more than 230,000 games with this move. Let's attack the knight now and expand in the center. [%csl Rc6]} 7. d5 Bxc3+ {Black is forced to trade on c3. We recapture.} (7... Nce7 {Black retreats the knight and keeps the knight on c3 pinned... [%csl Rc3]} 8. Qa4+ {But we win a piece! Good game. [%cal Ga4b4] [%csl Re8,Rb4]}) 8. bxc3 Nce7 {Black retreats.} 9. h3 {Here, h3 is a nice move to prevent the bishop from pinning our knight. We have a nice position here with a solid and a spacious center. Bd3 with O-O coming. [%cal Gf1d3,Ge1h1,Rc8g4] [%csl Gd5,Ge4]}) (6... d5 {There are more than 120,000 games in this variation. That means that there's a high chance you might see this. We will recapture because there's no danger to having Black's queen on d5. [%csl Re4]} 7. exd5 Qxd5 {The queen recaptures. Since the knight is pinned, this is safe for them. [%csl Rc3]} 8. Be2 {We will castle. Be2 is also a great square because we make sure our knight doesn't get pinned. We'll put a rook on the e-file, and play a3 to see if we get the bishop pair. [%cal Ge1g1,Ga2a3] [%csl Gf3]}) 7. d5 Ne7 {Black retreats. With Bd3 we develop a piece, protect the pawn on e4 and prepare to castle. [%csl Re4]} (7... Nxe4 {Black ignores the threat on the c6-knight and captures on e4, putting pressure on c3. Qc2 defends everything! [%csl Rc3]} 8. Qc2 Nxc3 {Black trades. We recapture, and now two pieces are under attack.} 9. bxc3 Qf6 {Black pins and attacks the c3-pawn. [%csl Ra1,Rc3]} 10. Bb2 {But Bb2 saves the day. We'll win a piece here as Black can't defend against all the threats. [%cal Rc3b4,Rd5c6] [%csl Gb2,Gc3,Rf6]}) 8. Bd3 O-O {Black castles. And we'll push even further! Our pawns are very flexible, gaining space with tempo.} (8... d6 {This is the most common move here. It happens in almost 50% of the games.} 9. Qa4+ {But it is a huge blunder! Qa4+ wins a piece. [%cal Ga4b4] [%csl Re8,Rb4]}) 9. e5 Nfxd5 {Looks like we've lost a pawn thanks to the pin on c3... ...but a bishop sacrifice saves the day! In fact, this is the Greek Gift sacrifice. [%csl Rh7]} 10. Bxh7+ Kxh7 {Black captures. A strong attack is coming after Ng5+. [%csl Rh7]} 11. Ng5+ Kg8 {Black retreats to "safety". But we are threatening checkmate. [%csl Rg8]} 12. Qh5 Re8 {Black makes an escape square for their king. Check.} 13. Qh7+ Kf8 {Only move. We check again.} 14. Qh8+ Ng8 {Black must block. But a check on h7 forces the king out to e7. This is a forced line after ...Kg8.} 15. Nh7+ Ke7 {The only move.} 16. O-O {And we simply castle here. Black is up a piece, but we are doing amazingly well here. Black's king isn't safe and we have many threats. For example, we are threatening the knight on d5, as well as capturing on g7 and Bg5! White is winning. [%cal Gh8g7,Gc3d5,Gc1g5] [%csl Rg7]} *

[Chapter "The Masters Main Line (3...d5)"]
[Study "Black Plays 4...dxe4"]
[Video "/downloader/downloads/The Ponziani/03-01 The Masters Main Line (3...d5) - Black Plays 4...dxe4.mp4"]
[Orientation "white"]

{In this last chapter on The Ponziani, we will learn how to play against 3...d5. This central pawn challenge is not so popular: only 7% of games, yet it's the most solid way of fighting against The Ponziani.}
1. e4 e5 {Black pushes their e-pawn, too. We attack the e5-pawn.} 2. Nf3 Nc6 {Black defends. And now we go for The Ponziani. [%csl Re5]} 3. c3 d5 {3...d5 has been played in more than 2 million games, but few players know the actual theory at sub-2000 levels. Black wants to challenge the center right away and hopes to activate the queen on d5 when we can't attack it with Nc3. We will respond with a pin on the c6-knight. Here, we also defend e4. [%csl Re4]} 4. Qa4 dxe4 {Nearly 47% of games go like this. White takes on e4. This move is really bad and shows that most people don't know the theory in this line. We won't recapture on e4. We will attack the pinned piece and take on e5. [%csl Rc6]} 5. Nxe5 Bd7 {5...Bd7 is the most popular: Black unpins and threatens the knight. However, we can take and then take on e4. We will be up a pawn.} (5... Qd5 {The second most popular move worth looking at is 5...Qd5. It is played in about 15% of games. With this move, Black defends their knight and attacks ours. But we can simply take and double the pawns. If 6...Bd7 we can play 7.Nb4. [%csl Gc6,Re5]} 6. Nxc6 bxc6 {Black has to recapture with the pawn. Can you see why? We can now develop and attack the queen.} 7. Bc4 Qd6 {Black goes to d6, which appears to be safe. We will castle kingside. This is the prelude to all the pressure we will place on them.} 8. O-O Nf6 {Black develops their knight, but now we can attack. We challenge the e4-pawn. [%csl Re4]} 9. d3 exd3 {Black takes on d3.} 10. Bf4 {Before Re1+, we have 10.Bf4 to attack the queen. Black can't capture because of Bxf7+. Our advantage increased quickly and we are much better. This is what happens if Black doesn't know how to handle 3...d5. We also have tactical ideas of Bxd3 where Black cannot take because of Qxc6+. [%cal Gf1e1,Gc4d3] [%csl Re8,Rd3]}) 6. Nxd7 Qxd7 {Black recaptures. And we are up a pawn.} 7. Qxe4+ Be7 {This line is an example of how play could continue. We push with d4 and gain more space. We've cleared the center so this is a must.} 8. d4 Nf6 {Black attacks our queen. But they do not have nearly enough compensation for the material. We are up a central pawn. [%csl Re4]} 9. Qf3 O-O {Castling kingside is bad.} (9... O-O-O {Castling queenside is also bad. Black is down material but they allow us to develop with tempo.} 10. Bd3 {Black is lost. We have a simple threat. If Black defends with something like ...g6, we can continue with O-O and Bf4. [%cal Gd3f5,Gc1f4,Ge1g1]}) 10. Be2 {We can block the e-file and get ready to castle. With our pawn advantage there's not much to strategize. Play the position and win, using the e-file and the e5-square to get a piece in the center. [%cal Ge1g1,Gb1d2,Gd2c4,Gc4e5,Gf1e1]} *

[Chapter "The Masters Main Line (3...d5)"]
[Study "Black Plays 4...Bd7 or 4...f6"]
[Video "/downloader/downloads/The Ponziani/03-02 The Masters Main Line (3...d5) - Black Plays 4...Bd7 or 4...f6.mp4"]
[Orientation "white"]

{For this second study, we will analyze what to play if Black actually knows the best theoretical moves: 4...Bd7 and 4...f6! We begin with 1.e4.}
1. e4 e5 {Black pushes their e-pawn, too. We attack the e5-pawn.} 2. Nf3 Nc6 {Black defends. And now we go for The Ponziani. [%csl Re5]} 3. c3 d5 {3...d5 challenges our e4-pawn. We will respond with a pin on the c6-knight. Here, we also defend e4. [%csl Re4]} 4. Qa4 Bd7 {4...Bd7 breaks the pin. It has been played in around 20% of games. But we can take on d5 since the discovered attack isn't a problem. [%csl Rd5]} (4... f6 {4...f6, played in around 12% of games, is cutting edge theory. Black supports the center with the pawn and isn't afraid of weakening their king. We will play 5.d3 to support e4. [%csl Gf6,Ge5]} 5. d3 dxe4 {5...dxe4 is natural. Black wants to trade. We recapture. Our bishop on f1 now has more squares to move to.} (5... Be6 {The bishop helps support d5 and it clears the way for castling queenside, potentially. Since our pawn is on d3, we can only develop to e2. [%cal Gd8d7,Ge8c8]} 6. Be2 Qd7 {Queen to d7 paves the way for queenside castling. These positions are generally equal because Black's center is solid. We castle.} (6... Bd6 {This is one of the potential setups that you could see. Both bishops out and supporting the central pawns. Our knight also supports e4. We can make use of e4 after all the central pawns are traded.} 7. Nbd2 Ne7 {Black develops the other knight to play O-O. Now breaking in the center is thematic. If you see that Black will castle kingside, d4 is a good plan.} 8. d4 exd4 {This trade also leads to another stable position. We recapture with the knight, centralizing it.} (8... dxe4 {8...dxe4 is possible, but it's not best. We will get our knight to e4 quicker. We take, with an attack on the bishop. [%csl Rd6]} 9. Nxe4 O-O {Black castles. We take in the center. Our knight is strong on e4, and so we don't want to take on d6 right away. We can do that later.} 10. dxe5 Nxe5 {Black takes on e5 with their knight.} 11. O-O {And we castle. We also don't mind ...Nxf3+, as we'll just recapture with the bishop. This is a stable position in which we'll enjoy some play, thanks to the incoming Rd1. [%cal Gf1d1]}) 9. Nxd4 Bd7 {The bishop goes and unpins the knight. But there isn't a scary discovered attack, so we can just castle. [%csl Rc6]} 10. O-O Nxd4 {Black can take on d4, attacking our queen. [%csl Ra4]} 11. Qxd4 {But we can simply recapture. This is an example of a line Black has to know in order to have a decent position in The Ponziani. All of our pieces are ready to handle any trades in the center and attacks. [%cal Gd4d3,Rd6e5]}) 7. O-O O-O-O {Black castles queenside, which falls right into our strategy.} 8. b4 {And one of the benefits of c3 is to play b4, too. We will attack the king that is now stuck on the queenside behind all its clumsy pieces. [%cal Gb4b5] [%csl Gc3,Gb4,Re6,Rd7,Rd8,Rc8,Ra7]}) 6. dxe4 Bd7 {This unpins the knight and prepares a discovered attack. [%csl Ra4]} 7. Qc2 {We of course get out of the way. We have an equal position on our hands but we will continue development with Bc4, O-O, Be3 and Nd2. [%cal Gf1c4,Gc1e3,Gb1d2]}) 5. exd5 Nd4 {5...Nd4 is played almost 72% of times. It's a discovered attack on the queen but we also have to take care of the c2-square. The d1-square is the only square for us to defend everything. [%csl Ra4,Rc2]} (5... Nce7 {This is played less often.} 6. Qb3 {We can fall back and pressure b7. We are up a pawn and have a substantial advantage. [%cal Gf1b5] [%csl Rb7,Re5]}) 6. Qd1 Nxf3+ {The knight takes on f3. We recapture with the queen.} 7. Qxf3 Nf6 {Black develops their knight to f6 with pressure on d5. ...Bg4 also becomes possible. That's why we must defend d5. [%csl Rd5]} 8. Bc4 Bg4 {Black attacks our queen. Luckily, our d5-pawn isn't completely defenseless. We can move away and attack the e5-pawn.} (8... Bd6 {With 8...Bd6, Black supports the e5-pawn and prepares castling. We push 9.d3 to control the e4-square. It will also be the funnel through which our pieces are developed.} 9. d3 O-O {Black castles.} 10. Bg5 {We don't mind giving up the bishop here because our control of the key e4-square will increase. We want to castle, play Nd2 and potentially even the move a4 if it's useful for queenside space and our bishop's comfort on c4. [%cal Gg5f6,Gb1d2,Ge1g1,Ga2a4] [%csl Rf6]}) (8... e4 {Our queen can be attacked by the pawn. On e2 we can exert pressure on the e4-pawn. [%csl Rf3]} 9. Qe2 Bd6 {Black brings the bishop out. We can push and gain some space because the e-pawn is pinned. Black is ahead in development for now, but our space advantage in the center restricts their potential.} 10. d4 O-O {Black castles. We'll play Bg5. Only good things can result from this pin. It's pressure that gives Black something to constantly worry about. [%csl Rf6]} 11. Bg5 h6 {If ...h6, we don't take on f6. We keep the pin and try to get Black to push ...g5. [%csl Rg5]} 12. Bh4 Re8 {The rook goes to e8 to defend e4 and potentially support the e3-push. [%csl Re4]} 13. O-O {And we castle. We want Nd2 and Rae1 because our rook on f1 may become useful if Black chooses to play ...e3 to open up the files. [%cal Gb1d2,Ga1e1,Gf2e3]}) 9. Qe3 Bd6 {When Black defends it, but they also block their queen's attack on d5. This is still better than giving us the e5-pawn.} 10. d3 {Once again, 10.d3 is the best way to develop here because our queen is rather restricted on e3. We have to defend e4 and make sure we have dominant control over that square. We want to get a knight to e4, castle and play h3. We have a good position. [%cal Gb1d2,Gd2e4,Gh2h3,Ge1g1]} *
